---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import Snippet from "../../components/Snippet.astro";

const toc = [
  { id: "what-this-is", label: "What this is" },
  { id: "the-loop", label: "The loop" },
  { id: "run-modes", label: "Run modes" },
];

const flow = `
iPhone --> Spectrum --> bridge --webhook--> Grok Bot
                                               |
iPhone <-- Spectrum <-- bridge <--send_reply---+
`;
---

<Docs
  title="How it works"
  eyebrow="Overview"
  description="A small Bun service that carries iMessages to the Grok Bot you already use, and brings its answer back to the same thread."
  toc={toc}
>
  <h2 id="what-this-is">What this is</h2>

  <p>
    grok-imessage is glue, not a brain. Your Bot keeps its memory, skills and cloud computer; this
    gives it an iMessage front door. <a href="https://photon.codes/docs/spectrum-ts">Spectrum</a> handles
    the iMessage line.
  </p>

  <h2 id="the-loop">The loop</h2>

  <Snippet lang="flow" code={flow} />

  <p>
    Grok Bot has no chat API, so the bridge wakes a routine over its webhook and hands it a one-shot
    reply token. When the Bot is done it calls <code>send_reply</code> over MCP, and the bridge sends the
    answer into the chat the token was bound to. Tokens work once and expire after 45 minutes.
  </p>

  <h2 id="run-modes">Run modes</h2>

  <p>The bridge does as much as you configure:</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Mode</th><th>Needs</th><th>Does</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>HTTP only</td><td>nothing</td><td>Serves <code>/health</code>, <code>/reply</code>, <code>/mcp</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Echo</td><td>Spectrum credentials</td><td>Texts come back as <code>echo: …</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Wake</td><td>+ webhook and public URL</td><td>Wakes the Bot and delivers its reply</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</Docs>
